# A Guided Laboratory for Forced Repetition in DNA

A four-letter alphabet has only finitely many motifs of a fixed length. Move the slider below to see how the code space grows and where repetition becomes unavoidable.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The governing fact is the [pigeonhole principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pigeonhole_principle): $n>q^m$ aligned samples cannot all be distinct. The first algorithm detects the collision constructively.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

## Typical collisions and absolute guarantees

Random samples usually collide near the birthday scale $q^{m/2}$, long before the worst-case deadline $q^m+1$. The plot distinguishes probability from certainty.

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the collision calculation</summary>
For $n$ independent uniform samples from $Q=q^m$ words, the no-collision probability is $\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}(1-i/Q)$ when $n\le Q$. Its complement is the collision probability. This model is probabilistic; the aligned collision theorem holds for every sequence.
</details>

## From pairs to repeated motif families

The same finite-fiber argument forces one motif to occur $r+1$ times once more than $rq^m$ blocks are sampled.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the multiplicity proof</summary>
If each of the $q^m$ words appeared at most $r$ times, there would be at most $rq^m$ samples. Therefore $n>rq^m$ forces a fiber of size at least $r+1$. Because distinct aligned blocks are disjoint, these are pairwise disjoint copies for positive motif length.
</details>

## Explore an actual sequence

Paste a DNA string and inspect its aligned blocks. This widget intentionally fixes the alignment, keeping the theorem's scope visible.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:0}}

## Window-uniform genomic repetition

For empirical work, the statistic $U_g(m,r)$ asks for the least window length such that every window contains one motif with $r$ disjoint occurrences. It can be computed by a monotone search.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal definitions and caveats</summary>
An aligned block starts at a multiple of $m$. A contiguous occurrence may start anywhere. A scattered occurrence uses increasing positions and may contain gaps. Every aligned collision gives a disjoint scattered collision, but the aligned bound need not be optimal for the larger pattern classes. For background on biological repeats, see the [genome overview](https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/fact-sheets/DNA-Fact-Sheet); for entropy, see [information theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_theory).
</details>

The interactive path moves from exact counting to random collision behavior and finally to a reproducible genome statistic, while preserving the distinction between a universal theorem and an empirical hypothesis.

